<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    Pulse — launcher icon fallback (API 24–25)
    ===
    Android 8.0 (API 26) introduced the <adaptive-icon> format.
    Devices on API 24–25 (Android 7.0 Nougat and 7.1 Nougat) do
    not understand that XML schema at all and will crash trying
    to inflate it.

    Resource resolution, however, is elegant here:
      - On API 26+: mipmap-anydpi-v26/ic_launcher.xml wins
        (higher specificity: -v26 qualifier matches and is more
        specific than the plain anydpi variant).
      - On API 24–25: mipmap-anydpi/ic_launcher.xml wins
        (this file; the -v26 variant is rejected because the
        device API is below 26).

    The two files present the SAME logical resource
    ("@mipmap/ic_launcher") to two different Android API
    versions, and each version gets a format it can inflate.

    Composition strategy for the fallback:
      <layer-list> with two <item> children renders in document
      order (first item is bottom, last item is top). We do:

        item 1 → solid pulse_accent background (bottom layer)
        item 2 → ic_launcher_foreground vector (top layer)

      The foreground vector is 108dp × 108dp with its "P" mark
      inside a 66dp centered safe zone. On API 24–25 the launcher
      will scale this 108dp vector down to the icon slot size
      (~48dp on most pre-Oreo devices). At that scale the "P"
      appears at roughly 60% of the icon width — a bit smaller
      than the ideal ~75% mark-to-frame ratio, but perfectly
      legible, and acceptable for the two API levels whose
      current installed base is well under 0.5% globally.

      We do NOT reference the adaptive foreground at 1:1 without
      a scale factor because the safe-zone padding built into
      that vector is only meaningful for API 26+ adaptive
      rendering. A future improvement — if the pre-Oreo install
      base ever justifies it — would be to add a dedicated
      "legacy" vector with tighter padding. That is not worth
      the extra file for the current audience.

    Result on API 24–25:
      A square icon (no adaptive mask on these API levels) with
      a solid pulse_accent background and a centered white "P".
      Looks intentional, matches the API 26+ icon's color
      scheme, and does not clash with any launcher style.
    ============================================================
-->
<layer-list xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
        Bottom layer: brand background.

        @color/pulse_accent resolves to a drawable automatically
        because AAPT2 auto-wraps any color resource as a solid-
        color drawable when a drawable is expected. There is no
        "incorrect type" here — this is a documented Android
        capability.
    -->
    <item android:drawable="@color/pulse_accent" />

    <!--
        Top layer: the "P" monogram.

        Same vector as the adaptive-icon foreground. Reusing the
        same file guarantees the two launcher icons never drift
        apart in artwork — if the P is redesigned, both API 24–25
        and API 26+ pick up the change from a single source of
        truth.
    -->
    <item android:drawable="@drawable/ic_launcher_foreground" />

</layer-list>
